  "query_preview": "../ISSUED AT 233 PM MST/ My concern when hoisting a \nflash flood watch 24 hours in advance during the monsoon is what \nhappens the day before and will there be considerable debris clouds \nstarting the morning to mess with the flash flood day forecast. Well \nthat happened this morning, an OH NO moment, with a considerable \ncloud deck and very light showers/virga across the area. This cloud \ndeck has been slowly eroding this afternoon with storms developing \nrather quick in the cloud breaks in a rather moist and unstable \natmosphere. The upper air pattern today is rather chaotic today with \nupper low over wrn TX and an upper high centered over NW AZ bringing \na northerly diffluent flow aloft across the area. In addition, there \nis an MCV west of Organ Pipe Ntl Mon that is developing storms in \nwestern Pima county.\n\nPWATs across the area ranged from 1.3\" near AZ/NM border to \nalmost 2\" over wrn AZ. The unstable atmosphere combined with the\nabove normal moisture will bring an increased flash flooding \nthreat to most of the area. Hourly rainfall rates from some of \nthe strongest storms could exceed 2\"/hour so some of the flash \nflooding could be considerable on a localized basis. Flash Flood\nWatch continues for Graham/Cochise counties thru 9 pm, and for \nPinal, Pima and Santa Cruz counties until 12 am Friday. The \nthreat for severe storms is limited with isolated gusts up to 55\nmph possible. \n\nActivity diminishing overnight with likely considerable cloud\ncover around Friday morning. Likely to have another slow start\nto the convection, with CAMs hitting the Intl border with heavy\nrain. \n\nThunderstorm chances continue daily this weekend and into next\nweek with day to day variability. Ensemble models show the \nupper level ridge becoming centered again over or near the Four \nCorners region next week keeping an active monsoon pattern\nacross the area.\n\nHigh temperatures over the next 7 days will remain below normal.",
